WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.119
Remember the helper with the clipboard?

2
00:00:05.119 --> 00:00:11.819
During the build, it finds every box with an @Inventory sticky note, reads the label, and writes a new card.

3
00:00:11.819 --> 00:00:14.176
Today, we write that helper ourselves.

4
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:21.606
In code, the helper is a processor: a class with one function, process, that KSP calls during the build.

5
00:00:21.606 --> 00:00:27.149
KSP hands it a Resolver, the clipboard, which answers questions about your code.

6
00:00:27.367 --> 00:00:34.770
Ask the clipboard for every symbol with the note, by its full name, package and all, and it answers: Lamp and Plant.

7
00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:37.833
The wardrobe has no note, so it stays out of it.

8
00:00:38.767 --> 00:00:42.074
Now here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

9
00:00:42.074 --> 00:00:44.587
You run one build, with one processor.

10
00:00:44.587 --> 00:00:47.497
So why would its process function run twice?

11
00:00:47.497 --> 00:00:48.820
Hold on to that one.

12
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.146
First, the writing.

13
00:00:51.146 --> 00:00:53.838
A card is just a file with text in it.

14
00:00:53.838 --> 00:00:59.292
KSP hands the processor a code generator, and createNewFile makes a new file.

15
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:10.131
Read it aloud: make a new file called LampInventoryCard.kt, in Lamp's package, note that it comes from Lamp's source file, and write the card's text into it.

16
00:01:10.333 --> 00:01:14.612
createNewFile hands back a stream, and the use block closes it.

17
00:01:14.612 --> 00:01:17.600
KSP picks the file up only once it's closed.

18
00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:21.838
A Java annotation processor does the same with javac's Filer.

19
00:01:21.838 --> 00:01:26.892
createSourceFile takes the new class's full name, and the class it came from.

20
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.424
Now look at the first argument, Dependencies.

21
00:01:30.424 --> 00:01:33.047
It names the source file this card came from.

22
00:01:33.047 --> 00:01:37.420
In the picture, the helper writes the box number in the corner of the card.

23
00:01:37.633 --> 00:01:42.571
Next weekend you repack one box, and only that box's card needs redoing.

24
00:01:42.571 --> 00:01:44.628
That's what the corner is for.

25
00:01:44.628 --> 00:01:47.851
Lesson 7 shows what goes wrong when it's blank.

26
00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:51.251
One small trap hides in the text itself.

27
00:01:51.251 --> 00:01:55.412
The card we generate is Kotlin, with its own template: dollar name.

28
00:01:55.412 --> 00:02:00.008
But the processor is Kotlin too, so its template would fill that in early.

29
00:02:00.233 --> 00:02:03.168
So we put a backslash before the second dollar.

30
00:02:03.168 --> 00:02:06.040
The backslash says: not my dollar, the card's.

31
00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:14.781
That's the first of several traps in writing code as plain strings, and lesson 4 trades the strings for a library that knows Kotlin's rules.

32
00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:20.434
Now let's run the whole processor on two classes, Lamp and Plant.

33
00:02:20.434 --> 00:02:23.346
It logs one line each time process runs.

34
00:02:23.346 --> 00:02:24.439
How many lines?

35
00:02:24.439 --> 00:02:25.604
Pause and guess.

36
00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:28.894
Two.

37
00:02:28.894 --> 00:02:31.685
Round 1: @Inventory on Lamp and Plant.

38
00:02:31.685 --> 00:02:34.403
Round 2: @Inventory on an empty list.

39
00:02:34.403 --> 00:02:38.589
Then the two cards it wrote, and the lamp's card, called.

40
00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.002
That's our answer.

41
00:02:40.002 --> 00:02:43.873
KSP calls process once per round, one pass over the files.

42
00:02:43.873 --> 00:02:52.217
A round that writes new files earns another round, so those files get read too, in case they carry sticky notes of their own.

43
00:02:52.433 --> 00:02:56.096
The new cards carry no notes, so round 2 finds nothing.

44
00:02:56.096 --> 00:03:00.692
Nothing new is written, processing ends, and the compiler takes over.

45
00:03:00.692 --> 00:03:01.691
The van leaves.

46
00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:03.504
Here the picture bends.

47
00:03:03.504 --> 00:03:07.828
A real helper would notice a new card the moment it's written.

48
00:03:07.828 --> 00:03:11.384
KSP's helper sees new files only in the next round.

49
00:03:11.384 --> 00:03:19.335
And it's the same helper every time: KSP creates the processor once per build, and calls process again each round.

50
00:03:20.267 --> 00:03:21.919
Now let's be extra careful.

51
00:03:21.919 --> 00:03:30.607
Instead of asking the clipboard for notes, a helper could walk through every file, every round, and write a card for each class with the note.

52
00:03:30.607 --> 00:03:31.892
More thorough, right?

53
00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.476
Round 1 sees Lamp.kt and writes its card.

54
00:03:35.476 --> 00:03:37.288
What does round 2 see?

55
00:03:37.288 --> 00:03:38.605
Pause and guess.

56
00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.926
Round 2 sees both files: Lamp.kt, again, and the new card.

57
00:03:45.926 --> 00:03:52.564
So the helper cheerfully starts Lamp's card a second time, and KSP stops the whole build.

58
00:03:52.564 --> 00:03:54.578
FileAlreadyExistsException.

59
00:03:54.578 --> 00:03:56.517
Exit code: internal error.

60
00:03:56.733 --> 00:03:58.327
Careful once is good.

61
00:03:58.327 --> 00:04:00.225
Careful twice is a crash.

62
00:04:00.225 --> 00:04:09.788
A file may be created only once per build, in KSP, and in javac too, whose Filer throws a FilerException for the same mistake.

63
00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:12.748
The clipboard protects you here.

64
00:04:12.748 --> 00:04:20.363
In each round, getSymbolsWithAnnotation returns only symbols from that round's new files, plus any set aside for later.

65
00:04:20.363 --> 00:04:23.819
That's why our real processor's round 2 found nothing.

66
00:04:24.033 --> 00:04:26.234
So, three habits for writing files.

67
00:04:26.234 --> 00:04:27.680
Write each output once.

68
00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.829
Ask the resolver for what's new in this round, not for everything.

69
00:04:31.829 --> 00:04:34.407
And name the source file in Dependencies.

70
00:04:35.333 --> 00:04:37.335
So, why did process run twice?

71
00:04:37.335 --> 00:04:42.671
Because writing a file earns another round, in case the new file carries a note.

72
00:04:42.671 --> 00:04:44.272
Round 1 wrote two cards.

73
00:04:44.272 --> 00:04:48.608
Round 2 read them, found nothing new, and the compiler took over.

74
00:04:48.833 --> 00:04:49.668
Quick check.

75
00:04:49.668 --> 00:04:52.591
The processor runs on Lamp, Plant and Box.

76
00:04:52.591 --> 00:04:56.000
How many rounds, and what does the last one find?

77
00:04:59.167 --> 00:04:59.960
Two rounds.

78
00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:06.455
Round 1 writes three cards, and round 2 finds an empty list, because cards carry no notes.
